/-!
# The inverse image of a locally principal closed subspace

Let `f : Y ⟶ X` be a morphism of algebraic spaces over a scheme `S` contained in a big fppf
site `B`, and let `Z ⊆ X` be a closed subspace.  Stacks 083X asserts that if `Z` is locally
principal — its defining ideal sheaf `𝓘_Z ⊆ 𝒪_X` is locally generated by one section on the
small étale site of `X`, Stacks 083B(1) — then so is the inverse image `f⁻¹(Z) ⊆ Y` of
Stacks 083Q.

The proof is by the chart computation of the inverse image.  On an étale scheme chart `V` of
`Y` the defining ideal of `f⁻¹(Z)` is the kernel of pullback of regular functions along the
canonical base change of `Z` formed along the *composite* `V ⟶ Y ⟶ X`
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_preimage`): the two base changes have the same range
as subfunctors of `V`, by `CategoryTheory.Subfunctor.preimage_comp`, hence cut out the same
closed subscheme.  If `V` moreover lies over an étale scheme chart `U` of `X` by a morphism `p`
of the site, and both charts are affine, then `Z ×_X V` is the base change of `Z ×_X U` along
`p`, so its ideal is the extension of the ideal of `Z ×_X U`:

`𝓘_{f⁻¹(Z)}(V) = p^♯(𝓘_Z(U)) · Γ(V)`,

which is `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_preimage_eq_map`.  That is the whole content
of the inverse image read on charts, and local principality follows from it chart by chart:
every point of a chart of `Y` has such a pair `(V, U)` above it with `𝓘_Z` generated on `U` by
a single section, and the pullback of that section generates `𝓘_{f⁻¹(Z)}` on `V`.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isLocallyPrincipal_preimage` is **Stacks 083X**.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_preimage` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.schemeKer_snd_preimage`: the defining ideal, respectively the ideal
  sheaf, of the inverse image on a chart of the source is that of the base change of `Z` along
  the composite chart.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.schemeKer_snd_comp`: the ideal sheaf of the base change of a closed
  subspace along a composite is the inverse image of the ideal sheaf of its base change along
  the second factor.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_preimage_eq_map`: on affine charts the defining
  ideal of the inverse image is the extension of the defining ideal of `Z`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_affine_chart_zeroLocus_definingIdealAt_eq`: for a locally
  principal closed subspace, every point of a chart lifts to an affine chart over it on which
  the zero locus of the defining ideal is the zero locus of a single global regular function.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_affine_chart_at`: every point of a chart lies in an affine
  open of it, which is again a chart.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_mem_sieve_scheme_point`: a covering sieve of the small étale
  site is jointly surjective on the scheme points of the chart.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_chart_pullback` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_chart_over`: the two base changes of charts used in the
  proof, with their point lifting.

## Implementation notes

The covering sieves of the small étale site are generated by the *chosen* covering families of
the big fppf site, which are not closed under the constructions made here, so no covering sieve
is exhibited by a family: each one is produced from the pointwise criterion
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.smallEtale_sieve_of_pointwise_covering`, and the sieves are taken to
be the largest ones with the property wanted of their members — the arrows on which the
inverse-image ideal is generated by one section, respectively on which a given section is a
multiple of the pullback of the generator (`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.multipleSieve`).  Both are
downward closed, the first because generating sections restrict with an unchanged index type
(`Stacks.SheafOfModules.generatingSectionsOverMap`) and the second because pullback of regular
functions is multiplicative, so only joint surjectivity on the scheme points of the chart has
to be checked.

A section of the defining ideal sheaf over an étale chart *is* a global regular function on the
chart scheme lying in the defining ideal there, but only up to unfolding the structure sheaf of
the small étale ringed site down to `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Γ`.  That identification is made
once, in `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionFun` and the lemmas beside it, and the computations
below are phrased entirely in terms of global functions; restriction, multiplication by a
regular function and equality of sections are each transported across it by one lemma.  The
algebra of the chart computation — a combination of pullbacks each of which becomes a multiple
of the generator after a further covering — is in turn separated into
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sum_mul_pullback_eq`, over an abstract composite `q`, and the two
purely ring-theoretic steps `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.map_mul_eq_of_mul_eq` and
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sum_mul_eq_map_sum`.

Generation by one section is used in the elementwise form
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.GeneratesLocally`, which the sheaf-theoretic form of Mathlib's
`SheafOfModules.GeneratingSections` is equivalent to by
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.generatesLocally_of_epi` and
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.generatorsOfGeneratesLocally`.  The elementwise form is what the chart
computation produces: a section of the inverse-image ideal on an affine chart is a *finite*
combination of pullbacks of sections of `𝓘_Z`, and each of those becomes a multiple of the
generator only after a further étale covering, so the coverings of the finitely many summands
have to be refined into one.  That refinement is an induction on the number of summands, and it
is why the hypothesis of generation is carried in a pointwise shape — on every chart over `U`,
every section of `𝓘_Z` is a multiple of the generator on some chart through which a prescribed
point lifts — rather than as a covering sieve.

The base changes of charts are formed in two ways, both of which keep the result a *scheme*
chart.  A chart `U` of `X` is pulled back to a chart of `Y` along a chart `V` of `Y` by
relative representability of `U ⟶ X` (`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_chart_over`), and a
morphism `U' ⟶ U` of charts of `X` is pulled back along a chart of `Y` lying over `U` by the
fibre product in the big fppf category (`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_chart_pullback`); in
both cases étaleness is stability of `AlgebraicGeometry.Etale` under base change and the chart
condition is part (1) of Stacks 03EC.  Points lift through either, in the second case by
surjectivity of the map from a fibre product of schemes to the fibre product of the point sets.

